53:["$","h2",null,{"className":"flex scroll-m-28 flex-row items-center gap-2","id":"core-challenge-capturing-temporal-dependencies","children":[["$","a",null,{"data-card":"","href":"#core-challenge-capturing-temporal-dependencies","className":"peer","children":"Core Challenge: Capturing Temporal Dependencies"}],["$","svg",null,{"ref":"$undefined","xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide size-3.5 shrink-0 text-fd-muted-foreground opacity-0 transition-opacity peer-hover:opacity-100","aria-label":"Link to section","children":[[["$","path","1cjeqo",{"d":"M10 13a5 5 0 0 0 7.54.54l3-3a5 5 0 0 0-7.07-7.07l-1.72 1.71"}],["$","path","19qd67",{"d":"M14 11a5 5 0 0 0-7.54-.54l-3 3a5 5 0 0 0 7.07 7.07l1.71-1.71"}]],"$undefined"]}]]}]
54:["$","p",null,{"children":"A feedforward neural network  as discussed in the previous lecture  assumes that all input features are independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.). This assumption breaks down in sequential settings. In sequence modeling:"}]
55:["$","ul",null,{"children":["\n",["$","li",null,{"children":"Inputs are ordered."}],"\n",["$","li",null,{"children":"Future predictions must respect and leverage past observations."}],"\n",["$","li",null,{"children":"The same model must be applied across time steps or positions."}],"\n"]}]
56:["$","p",null,{"children":"To handle this, we need architectures that are designed to process sequences  capable of learning representations where current predictions are functions of both present and past information."}]
57:["$","p",null,{"children":["This sets the stage for understanding Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs), Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks, and eventually, Transformers.\n",["$","$L67",null,{"sizes":"(max-width: 768px) 100vw, (max-width: 1200px) 70vw, 900px","src":"./image_rnn_1.png","alt":"This is an ALT text","className":"rounded-lg"}],"\n",["$","$L67",null,{"sizes":"(max-width: 768px) 100vw, (max-width: 1200px) 70vw, 900px","src":"./image_rnn_2.png","alt":"This is an ALT text","className":"rounded-lg"}],"\n",["$","$L67",null,{"sizes":"(max-width: 768px) 100vw, (max-width: 1200px) 70vw, 900px","src":"./image_rnn_3.png","alt":"This is an ALT text","className":"rounded-lg"}],"\n",["$","$L67",null,{"sizes":"(max-width: 768px) 100vw, (max-width: 1200px) 70vw, 900px","src":"./image_rnn_4.png","alt":"This is an ALT text","className":"rounded-lg"}],"\n",["$","$L67",null,{"sizes":"(max-width: 768px) 100vw, (max-width: 1200px) 70vw, 900px","src":"./image_rnn_5.png","alt":"This is an ALT text","className":"rounded-lg"}]]}]
58:["$","h1",null,{"className":"flex scroll-m-28 flex-row items-center gap-2","id":"chapter-2-27-from-static-networks-to-time-aware-models","children":[["$","a",null,{"data-card":"","href":"#chapter-2-27-from-static-networks-to-time-aware-models","className":"peer","children":"Chapter :2 2.7 From Static Networks to Time-Aware Models"}],["$","svg",null,{"ref":"$undefined","xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide size-3.5 shrink-0 text-fd-muted-foreground opacity-0 transition-opacity peer-hover:opacity-100","aria-label":"Link to section","children":[[["$","path","1cjeqo",{"d":"M10 13a5 5 0 0 0 7.54.54l3-3a5 5 0 0 0-7.07-7.07l-1.72 1.71"}],["$","path","19qd67",{"d":"M14 11a5 5 0 0 0-7.54-.54l-3 3a5 5 0 0 0 7.07 7.07l1.71-1.71"}]],"$undefined"]}]]}]
59:["$","p",null,{"children":"In the previous section, we established the importance of modeling sequential dependencies. We now turn to how the field historically addressed this problem, beginning with a shift from traditional feedforward architectures to networks capable of handling temporal dynamics."}]
5a:["$","h2",null,{"className":"flex scroll-m-28 flex-row items-center gap-2","id":"revisiting-the-perceptron","children":[["$","a",null,{"data-card":"","href":"#revisiting-the-perceptron","className":"peer","children":"Revisiting the Perceptron"}],["$","svg",null,{"ref":"$undefined","xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide size-3.5 shrink-0 text-fd-muted-foreground opacity-0 transition-opacity peer-hover:opacity-100","aria-label":"Link to section","children":[[["$","path","1cjeqo",{"d":"M10 13a5 5 0 0 0 7.54.54l3-3a5 5 0 0 0-7.07-7.07l-1.72 1.71"}],["$","path","19qd67",{"d":"M14 11a5 5 0 0 0-7.54-.54l-3 3a5 5 0 0 0 7.07 7.07l1.71-1.71"}]],"$undefined"]}]]}]
5b:["$","p",null,{"children":["Let us return briefly to the basic building block introduced in Lecture 1: the perceptron. Each perceptron takes a vector of inputs ",["$","span",null,{"className":"katex-error","title":"ParseError: KaTeX parse error: Can't use function '\\(' in math mode at position 1: \\̲(̲ x_1, x_2, \\dot…","style":{"color":"#cc0000"},"children":"\\( x_1, x_2, \\dots, x_m \\)"}],", applies a weighted sum, and passes the result through a non-linear activation function to produce an output ",["$","span",null,{"className":"katex-error","title":"ParseError: KaTeX parse error: Can't use function '\\(' in math mode at position 1: \\̲(̲ \\hat{y} \\)","style":{"color":"#cc0000"},"children":"\\( \\hat{y} \\)"}],". This output can then feed into further layers to construct deep networks.",["$","br",null,{}],"\n","In its basic form, the perceptron operates on a fixed-length vector with no awareness of any sequential structure. All inputs are assumed to belong to a single time slice. There is no notion of temporal ordering or dependence on previous data points. This limitation makes such architectures insufficient for handling sequential data."]}]
5c:["$","h2",null,{"className":"flex scroll-m-28 flex-row items-center gap-2","id":"applying-feedforward-networks-to-time-series-a-naive-attempt","children":[["$","a",null,{"data-card":"","href":"#applying-feedforward-networks-to-time-series-a-naive-attempt","className":"peer","children":"Applying Feedforward Networks to Time Series: A Naive Attempt"}],["$","svg",null,{"ref":"$undefined","xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide size-3.5 shrink-0 text-fd-muted-foreground opacity-0 transition-opacity peer-hover:opacity-100","aria-label":"Link to section","children":[[["$","path","1cjeqo",{"d":"M10 13a5 5 0 0 0 7.54.54l3-3a5 5 0 0 0-7.07-7.07l-1.72 1.71"}],["$","path","19qd67",{"d":"M14 11a5 5 0 0 0-7.54-.54l-3 3a5 5 0 0 0 7.07 7.07l1.71-1.71"}]],"$undefined"]}]]}]
5d:["$","p",null,{"children":["One naive way to apply a static neural network to sequence data is to treat each time step independently. Imagine rotating our input-output diagram vertically, where each input      ",["$","span",null,{"className":"katex-error","title":"ParseError: KaTeX parse error: Can't use function '\\(' in math mode at position 1: \\̲(̲ x_t \\)","style":{"color":"#cc0000"},"children":"\\( x_t \\)"}]," is processed by the same network ",["$","span",null,{"className":"katex-error","title":"ParseError: KaTeX parse error: Can't use function '\\(' in math mode at position 1: \\̲(̲ f \\)","style":{"color":"#cc0000"},"children":"\\( f \\)"}]," to yield an output",["$","span",null,{"className":"katex-error","title":"ParseError: KaTeX parse error: Can't use function '\\(' in math mode at position 1: \\̲(̲ \\hat{y}_t \\)","style":{"color":"#cc0000"},"children":"\\( \\hat{y}_t \\)"}],". We repeat this process across all time steps ",["$","span",null,{"className":"katex-error","title":"ParseError: KaTeX parse error: Can't use function '\\(' in math mode at position 1: \\̲(̲ t = 0, 1, 2, \\…","style":{"color":"#cc0000"},"children":"\\( t = 0, 1, 2, \\dots \\)"}]]}]
5e:["$","p",null,{"children":["While this allows us to handle sequences of inputs, each prediction ",["$","span",null,{"className":"katex-error","title":"ParseError: KaTeX parse error: Can't use function '\\(' in math mode at position 1: \\̲(̲ \\hat{y}_t \\)","style":{"color":"#cc0000"},"children":"\\( \\hat{y}_t \\)"}]," is based only on the corresponding ",["$","span",null,{"className":"katex-error","title":"ParseError: KaTeX parse error: Can't use function '\\(' in math mode at position 1: \\̲(̲ x_t \\)","style":{"color":"#cc0000"},"children":"\\( x_t \\)"}],". There is no memory of what happened at earlier time steps. This independence assumption contradicts the very premise of sequential modeling, which relies on the idea that prior context informs future states.",["$","br",null,{}],"\n","This setup lacks the ability to model dependencies across time. For example, if you are predicting a word in a sentence, the words that came before are crucial to predicting the next one. Ignoring previous steps leads to poor modeling capacity in such domains."]}]
